\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Plot of the Wigner Semi-Circle distribution.}}{25}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Plot of $F_{TW}^{(2)}(x)$, the Tracy-Widom distribution.}}{26}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Plot of a simulation of a Coulomb gas in two dimensions under a potential with threefold symmetry. In black crosses we plotted one sample from the simulation. In shadowy dots, the \enquote {averaged} samples, suggesting the theoretical equilibrium region.}}{29}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Plot of the Wigner Semi-Circle distribution normalized such that $\supp {\mu ^{(SC)}} = [-1, 1]$.}}{38}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The dashed line is the Semi-circle distribution, whereas in solid we display the average distribution of eigenvalues from $\hat M_n$. The columns correspond to $n = 10,50,100$ from left to right. The lines correspond to the GOE, GUE and GSE from top to bottom. We took $10000$ matrix samples for each average distribution.}}{39}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces For all graphs, the line is the average distribution of eigenvalues for the GUE. The columns correspond to the polynomial order (and matrix size) $n = 10,50,100$ from left to right. The dashes in the axis correspond to the roots of the $n$-th Hermite polynomial.}}{49}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces $20$ Non-intersecting Brownian paths for $0 \leq t \leq 1$ s.t. they start and end at $y=0.$}}{54}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Three conditions simulated form the $20$ Dyson Bridges taken from $t = 0.1, 0.5, 0.9$. For each one the experimental density (line plot) and one sampled position (dashes in axis) were plotted. The three plots are re-scaled by $((2t(1-t))\sqrt {N}).$}}{55}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Shown in the shadow is the mean configurations - in some an approximation of the limit distribution of points, and in the scatter is a random $n = 50$ sample from the distribution. We show configurations for combinations of the parameters $t = 0, 0.5, 1, 2$ from left ro right and $a = 2, 3, 5$ from top to bottom.}}{59}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Four conditions for the Monic potential, the equilibrium measure is expressed by the line plot. For all cases we used $t=1$ and set $m=1,2,3,5$ from left to right. The bin-plot is the result of the simulation of the equivalent Coulomb Gas taken from \citeonline {TCCJoaoPimenta}. }}{64}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces The figure depicts the two topological cases for the support of the equilibrium measure - the droplet. On the left $r_0 > 0$ defining the annulus and on the right $r_0 =0$ defining the disk.}}{72}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces In the Plot it is represented the plot for $\ee ^{- \lambda (\cosh (x)-1)}$ and an increasing $\lambda = 1, 2, 10, 70$ - darker represent larger values of the parameter. The dotted patter represents $\ee ^{-\lambda x^2}$ for large lambda.}}{81}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces The lines represent the solution to $\mathop {\textrm Im}[f(z) - f(t_c)] = 0$ for the three possible cases of the Hermite Polynomial. the cases are from left to right $\alpha \in [0,2)$, $\alpha = 2$ and $\alpha > 2$. Only for the first case the critical points are complex. These are the curves we use to perform the asymptotic analysis on the integral. Note the case $\alpha = 2$; the coalescence of critical point gives origin to the Airy function.}}{93}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Graphs for the two cases of the function $rq'(r)$. On the left, we show the case for $r_0 > 0$, corresponding to the annulus droplet. On the right, we show the case for $r_0 = 0$, corresponding to the disk droplet.}}{98}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces The figure depicts an annulus referring to the equilibrium measure when $r_0 > 0$. As the complex plane integral is reduced to a line integral, we draw the domain change and the point of interest $r_\tau $ where $V$ has a critical point. Around this point we depict the region around the minima, which in the method that should account for the main order of the integral we solve for.}}{100}{figure.caption.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces The figure depicts an disk referring to the equilibrium measure when $r_0 = 0$. As the complex plane integral is reduced to a line integral, we draw the domain change and the point of interest $r_\tau $ where $V$ has a critical point. Around this point we depict the region around the minima, which in the method that should account for the main order of the integral we solve for. The left image corresponds to the case when $r_{j/n} >> n^{-\epsilon }$ and the right image corresponds to $r_{j/n} << n^{-\epsilon }$. In this case $r*_{\tau }$ will define the contributing region to the integral.}}{112}{figure.caption.32}%
